WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.817
Welcome to SaleLedger. This short tour uses fictional practice files, so nothing here is a

00:00:06.817 --> 00:00:13.255
real auction house. To begin, go to getsaleledger.com and click Start free month.

00:00:13.255 --> 00:00:20.072
Checkout is handled by Stripe. A card is required, but nothing is charged for thirty days,

00:00:20.072 --> 00:00:22.420
and you can cancel before then.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:30.252
After checkout, you land on this page. It shows your license key. Copy it somewhere safe.

00:00:30.252 --> 00:00:36.984
We also keep it with your subscription, so you can come back to this page any time to see

00:00:36.984 --> 00:00:41.900
it again. The download button for the Windows app is right below.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.888
Double-click SaleLedger.exe. It opens in your web browser, on your own computer. Paste

00:00:49.888 --> 00:00:56.547
your key and click Activate. That key is the only thing SaleLedger ever sends anywhere.

00:00:56.547 --> 00:01:02.900
Your ledger, your bank statement, and your consignor data never leave this machine.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.702
Each month you need three files. One: a settlement export from Auction Flex. The plain

00:01:10.702 --> 00:01:17.639
Consignor Summary, Consignment Order totals, or item-level totals all work; use whichever

00:01:17.639 --> 00:01:24.575
report you already run. Two: the QuickBooks IIF file that Auction Flex's Pay Consignors

00:01:24.575 --> 00:01:31.355
tool exports. Three: your trust account bank statement, as CSV, Excel, or your bank's

00:01:31.355 --> 00:01:32.680
Quicken download.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:40.186
Under Option A, choose the three files. Then enter the auction number, the trust account's

00:01:40.186 --> 00:01:46.449
opening balance on the first day of the period, and the as-of date. In the practice set,

00:01:46.449 --> 00:01:52.641
that's auction 246, an opening balance of one hundred fifty thousand dollars,

00:01:52.641 --> 00:01:56.200
and August thirty-first. Click Run reconciliation.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:03.193
The result is one line: In balance, or Out of balance. Below it, the three numbers that

00:02:03.193 --> 00:02:09.154
have to agree: the bank's adjusted balance, the trust control balance from your records,

00:02:09.154 --> 00:02:15.116
and the total owed to consignors. In the practice month they don't agree, and the report

00:02:15.116 --> 00:02:17.080
says so instead of hiding it.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:24.458
Every gap becomes a numbered exception in plain English. Here, a consignor check cleared

00:02:24.458 --> 00:02:30.665
for five hundred dollars more than the ledger recorded. Further down, a deposit with no

00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:36.372
matching auction, and a transfer to the operating account with no authorization.

00:02:36.372 --> 00:02:41.580
SaleLedger never guesses at an explanation. It tells you what to look at.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:48.623
The report ends with a check register: date, payee, check number, and amount. That is

00:02:48.623 --> 00:02:54.776
exactly what state bookkeeping rules like Wisconsin's ask for. Keep the report with your

00:02:54.776 --> 00:02:59.600
monthly records, and you're audit-ready without doing anything extra.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:07.575
Next month, SaleLedger pre-fills the opening balance from the bank's actual ending balance

00:03:07.575 --> 00:03:14.145
last period, so you never retype it. Bring in three new files, click Run, and read the

00:03:14.145 --> 00:03:18.270
result. That's the whole routine. Thanks for watching.
